slurm-spank-plugin-ebpf-exporter for Debian/Scibian
==========================================

Files installed
---------------

  /usr/lib/slurm/spank_ebpf.so           the plugin itself
  /etc/slurm/plugstack.conf.d/ebpf.conf  its plugstack declaration (conffile)

Upstream targets the RPM layout (/usr/lib64/slurm). The Debian build installs
into /usr/lib/slurm and rewrites the path inside ebpf.conf accordingly. If the
local Slurm build looks for its plugins somewhere else, change SLURM_LIBDIR at
the top of debian/rules and rebuild.

Enabling the plugin
-------------------

Slurm only reads plugstack.conf.d if /etc/slurm/plugstack.conf includes it:

  include /etc/slurm/plugstack.conf.d/*.conf

That line is not shipped by this package, since plugstack.conf belongs to the
Slurm packages. Add it, then restart slurmd on the compute nodes. The load
shows up in the slurmd log:

  spank: /etc/slurm/plugstack.conf.d/ebpf.conf:1: Loaded plugin spank_ebpf.so

The two modes
-------------

The shipped ebpf.conf enables "always" mode: every job that starts on the node
turns ebpf_exporter on. To restrict monitoring to jobs submitted with --ebpf,
comment that line and uncomment the opt-in one:

  # required /usr/lib/slurm/spank_ebpf.so mode=always
  optional /usr/lib/slurm/spank_ebpf.so mode=opt-in

Opt-in mode needs this package installed and declared on submission hosts as
well (login nodes, wherever sbatch/salloc run), not only on compute nodes.
Without it sbatch rejects --ebpf as an unrecognized option before the job is
even submitted, because the option is registered by the plugin itself.

Changing the mode does not require rebuilding the package: edit the conffile
and restart slurmd.

Runtime expectations
--------------------

ebpf_exporter must be installed on the compute nodes but left disabled at boot
(systemctl disable ebpf_exporter): the plugin owns its lifecycle. It calls
systemctl and uses flock(2) on /run/ebpf_exporter.refcount, so systemd and
util-linux are needed on the node.

